# APML v1.0.0 - PlayerConductor (SimplePlayer transition ownership)
# SSI Learning App
# Last Updated: 2026-07-21

spec player_conductor_architecture:
  version: "1.0.0"
  status: "implemented — playback lifecycle, round-boundary interlude, and
    skip/jump seek paths migrated. Full cachedRounds/loadedRounds mirror
    deletion (Option 2's other named target) is NOT done — see
    docs/player-decomposition-options.md and open_follow_ups below."
  date: "2026-07-21"

  source: "packages/player-vue/src/playback/PlayerConductor.ts"
  decision_doc: "docs/player-decomposition-options.md (Option 2)"

  description: |
    Seven user-reported player bugs on 2026-07-21 traced to one disease:
    the player is a de-facto distributed state machine coordinated by
    ad-hoc async code, with no single owner of "what state is playback in
    right now, and who's allowed to change it". PlayerConductor is that
    owner — the only code allowed to call SimplePlayer's
    play/pause/resume/stop/skipRound/stepCycle/skipToPhase/jumpToRound/
    addRounds/appendRounds/replaceQueueFromCurrent. LearningPlayer.vue
    never imports SimplePlayer directly (verified — only
    composables/useSimplePlayer.ts constructs it); useSimplePlayer routes
    every one of its methods through the conductor, so all ~80 call sites
    in LearningPlayer.vue get the guarantees below for free, with zero
    changes required at most of those call sites.

  disease_and_fix: |
    Four recurring failure modes (docs/player-decomposition-options.md
    "The actual disease"), each retired structurally, not just made less
    likely:
      - fire-and-forget promises stranding the player paused (ed738a0f) →
        runInterlude's bounded exit (try/finally + timeout).
      - async work racing the engine across a skip/jump (73c1507a) →
        runSeek's seeking(intent) state pauses BEFORE any async prefetch,
        never after.
      - state mirrors drifting (loadedRounds vs the engine's own queue) →
        NOT retired by the conductor (separate, larger migration — see
        open_follow_ups). One additional live instance (the adaptation-v2
        breather round) was found and fixed while auditing for this work.
      - silent stale-cache fallbacks deciding policy invisibly → out of
        scope (the offline layer, not playback transitions).

  states:
    loading: "Pre-first-play / just stopped. Not itself bounded by a timeout — exit is a user/data event (play()), not an in-flight async op the conductor owns."
    playing: "Stable — engine is actively playing."
    userPaused: "Stable — engine is paused by user/explicit choice."
    ended: "Set on the engine's session_complete event."
    interlude(kind, resumeTo): |
      Transient. Brackets an async interlude (commentary / pod lap / L1
      cup) around a pause. The body (fn passed to runInterlude) keeps its
      OWN internal pause()/resume() decisions — including deliberately
      landing paused (e.g. learner pressed stop mid-lap) — trusted on
      SUCCESS. Only on FAILURE (thrown error or the timeout bound) does
      the conductor force a fallback: resume if resumeTo was 'playing'.
      Serialized: a second interlude/seek queues behind an in-flight one.
    seeking(intent): |
      Transient. Brackets an async seek (skip/jump prep — e.g. prefetching
      destination audio) around a pause. intent is captured ONCE at entry
      and restored on landing regardless of success/error (bounded exit).
      NOT FIFO-serialized against other seeks — cancel-and-replace via a
      generation counter, so a rapid double-tap doesn't stall behind the
      first tap's full timeout; the superseded seek's own landing is
      skipped (isStale() flips true), the newest seek owns it.

  api:
    request: "(engine => void) => void — direct, synchronous, always-immediate engine control. Used internally by useSimplePlayer's play/pause/resume/stop/skip*/jumpToRound/addRounds/appendRounds/replaceQueueFromCurrent, and safe to call from INSIDE an interlude/seek body (never touches the serialization queue, so it can't deadlock against itself)."
    runInterlude: "(kind, fn, opts?) => Promise<void> — see interlude(kind, resumeTo) above. Exposed as simplePlayer.runInterlude in the composable."
    runSeek: "(fn(isStale), opts?) => Promise<T|undefined> — see seeking(intent) above. Exposed as simplePlayer.runSeek in the composable."

  dev_guard: |
    installDevGuard (dev/test builds only, import.meta.env.DEV or
    MODE==='test') shadows SimplePlayer's control methods on the INSTANCE
    to flag any call that bypasses the conductor (isExecuting false at
    call time) via console.error with a stack — non-blocking, a
    visibility net for the incremental migration, not a hard gate.

  migrated_call_sites:
    - "useSimplePlayer.ts — every control method now calls conductor.request() instead of the raw player instance (2026-07-21)."
    - "LearningPlayer.vue handleRoundBoundary — wraps handleRoundBoundaryBody in conductor.runInterlude('round-boundary', ..., {timeoutMs: 10 * 60 * 1000}). Same try/catch recovery contract as the ed738a0f fix; the timeout is new (a hung promise — never throws, never resolves — previously had no recovery path at all)."
    - "LearningPlayer.vue prepareAndJump (round/cycle skip) — wraps the prefetch+jump body in conductor.runSeek. skipPrepToken (dialog visibility + unmount cancellation) is kept as a SEPARATE UI-layer staleness signal alongside runSeek's own isStale()."
    - "LearningPlayer.vue handleSkipToBelt (belt skip) — wraps the load+jump body in conductor.runSeek. Previously had NO supersede protection for a rapid double-tap (unlike prepareAndJump's token); runSeek's cancel-and-replace closes that gap for free."

  open_follow_ups: |
    - Full loadedRounds/cachedRounds mirror deletion: ~90 read/write touch
      points in LearningPlayer.vue, several with genuinely distinct
      semantics (live engine mirror vs. text-only metadata mirror vs.
      preview-mode snapshot) that need per-site understanding before a
      safe rewrite — a separate, scoped migration. Audited all
      addRounds/appendRounds/replaceQueueFromCurrent call sites for this
      round; all but one were already correctly synced or deliberately
      decoupled by explicit code-comment design.
    - Plan 019 (docs/... LearningPlayer.vue region extraction) resumes as
      the follow-on once this settles further, per the decision doc's
      recommended sequencing.
